WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.750
I matched the test marks to

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.410
my class list by name.

3
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.770
Two Fatimas, one Fatimah, and a

4
00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:10.230
boy who left last term.

5
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.140
Four mistakes in thirty rows!

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.380
Today: one ID for every pupil,

7
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.160
and joining sheets by it.

8
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.980
Names make poor keys.

9
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.380
Two pupils share a name, spellings

10
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.280
change, and families move.

11
00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.550
A pupil number, given once and never

12
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.130
reused, means one pupil and only one.

13
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.170
That number is a key: a column whose

14
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.330
value picks out exactly one row.

15
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.090
Put the key in every sheet about pupils,

16
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.270
and you can match any two of them.

17
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.470
In a spreadsheet, a lookup

18
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:50.830
joins two sheets.

19
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.360
For each mark, XLOOKUP or VLOOKUP finds

20
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:57.940
the pupil with the same ID in the class

21
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.760
list, and brings back the name.

22
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.940
When there's no match, it

23
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.020
shows an error instead.

24
00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:05.950
Those errors are useful.

25
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.080
A mark with no pupil means an ID was typed

26
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.180
wrong, or the list is missing someone.

27
00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.250
A pupil with no mark was

28
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.210
absent, or the mark got lost.

29
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.410
Check them; never just delete them.

30
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.060
In Python, a dictionary does the lookup.

31
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.340
Store each pupil's name under their ID.

32
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.010
Then, for each mark, get the name for its

33
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.730
ID, or a question mark when there's none.

34
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:34.410
Quick check!

35
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.570
Why should we match the marks to the class

36
00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.270
list by pupil ID, and not by name?

37
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.420
Names repeat and change spelling; an

38
00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.960
ID belongs to one pupil only.

39
00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.070
That's what makes it a key.

40
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.770
Give the number once, and

41
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.090
use it on every sheet.

42
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.680
In the app, pupils are on one side

43
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.340
and yesterday's maths marks on the

44
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.820
other, matched by number.

45
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.740
Try each join.

46
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.200
Who disappears, who appears twice, and

47
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.120
where do the empty cells come from?

48
00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.050
Your turn.

49
00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.150
After the lookup, one mark

50
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:15.730
has no matching pupil.

51
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.890
What does that tell me?

52
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.890
An ID was typed wrong, or the list

53
00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.690
is missing someone: check it.

54
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.780
Remember: names make poor keys,

55
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.440
so give every pupil one ID.

56
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.480
Join sheets with a lookup by that ID.

57
00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.330
And treat every missing match as a

58
00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.950
question, not as rubbish.

59
00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.730
Try it in class tomorrow:

60
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.130
play “Human join”.

61
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.520
Half the class holds class-list cards,

62
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.180
with a number and a name; the other half

63
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.800
holds mark cards, with a number.

64
00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.360
Pupils find their partner by number.

65
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.490
Who's left without a partner, and why?
